- `rebuildWindow(matchId, fromTick, toTick)`: rebuild from initial state, verify frames 1..toTick contiguous + hashes, return cloned states for ticks fromTick..toTick.
- Snapshot: status = tick >= frontier ? current.status : 'running'.
- Deltas: changed = diff of public projections between consecutive rebuilt states; casualties recomputed (deaths); settled from recorded frame; team_damage from rebuilt state; status per tick rule; self-check applying deltas to base reproduces next hash.

For the self-check in deltas: build applied state from base clone, apply each delta's changed units (strip `alive`), set teamDamage and tick, then hash and compare with the hash of the final state (which was verified against recorded frame). If deltas empty, applied == base, hash == base hash, and next_tick == after_tick — compare with base hash. Good.

Wait, one subtlety on the self-check: applied.units[id] = projection without `alive`. Projection includes attacking etc. The internal unit shape is {attacking, health, id, position, team, velocity} — same minus alive. Good.

Another subtlety: stateHash treats dead units with attacking:false, health:null regardless of stored values. The rebuilt state keeps raw values; hash computed on raw state is what was verified. Applied state uses same raw values → same hash. Good.

Now, `changed` diff: compare prev and next unit projections. Fields: alive (derived from health), attacking, health, position, team, velocity. team never changes but include in comparison anyway — cheap.

Note the spec: "changed 按 unit id 排序，列出相对上一帧公开字段有变化的单位及变化后的完整投影；仅 attacking 改变或单位死亡也不能遗漏，无单位变化则为空。" So changed entries = full projection (same shape as snapshot units: alive, attacking, health, id, position, team, velocity). Sorted by id.

Delta object field order: tick, state_hash, status, settled, casualties, team_damage, changed (per spec listing).

Snapshot response: match_id, tick, status, state_hash, team_damage, units.

Deltas response: match_id, after_tick, base_state_hash, next_tick, complete, deltas.

